From 0aacbef50faa83a449b439d56d985dafe08cd34b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LibreELEC Hades <dev@localhost>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 09:24:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: the level 5 offsets of a video as a setting
 (automatic, direct, or left aside)

What the player does with the level 5 offsets of a video's metadata --
read them and describe the picture as it is placed on the screen, blank
the bars of the raster itself, and take the edges drawn over out of them
-- was the only behaviour, decided by the bridge. videoscreen.hadesdvl5
(System > Display, Dolby Vision, expert) makes it the user's:

- automatic, as before (the default): the offsets read as offsets of the
  picture and of a 3840x2160 master, mapped to the picture's place;
- direct: the offsets sent as the RPU gives them, nothing of the picture
  blanked and no edge taken out -- right where the screen shows the
  video pixel for pixel, wrong wherever it is scaled or zoomed, and
  subtitles drawn over the bars are masked by the display;
- left aside: the area of the picture's place sent, as for metadata
  without a level 5 block.

The setting is read when a native output starts, as the trims are
(CDVEngineSettings::l5). In the two new modes the margins of a frame are
the picture's place, so the player blanks nothing and renders as
metadata without a level 5 block; the cover of the level 5 block (what
is drawn over the bars) is not decided and the measurement of the
pictures against the offsets does not run. The left-aside mode leaves
the video's own decision alone: a change back to automatic takes the
offsets as they are.

kodi.log says once a video which way its offsets go out.

Tested on the host (l5_test.c, not part of the patch): a 1920x1080
picture placed at (960,540) in the raster with offsets 270 272 274 276
-- automatic maps them to the smaller of the two readings (1095 1096
677 678) and takes a covered edge out; direct sends them as they are,
with the margins of the picture's place, whatever is drawn over, and
keeps the mode through a restart; left aside sends that place and makes
no decision of the video; a 3840x2160 picture filling the raster keeps
the bytes it always had.
